            <ArticleTitle>Inguinal hernia incarceration as a form of intestinal tuberculosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1097-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report the case of a 41 year-old male who came to the emergency room with a complaint of abdominal pain, and was diagnosed to have an acute obstructive abdomen due to a right inguinal hernia incarceration. During surgery, an intestinal granulomatous inflammation was observed adhered to the hernial sac. The histopathologic study confirmed the diagnosis of tuberculosis. We present a review of the different clinical forms of intestinal tuberculosis and the difficulties encountered in the differential diagnosis of such, emphasizing the uncommon presentation described in our patient.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
